This isn't an 99 issue. It works fine with local models. You have to configure opencode a little more. The reason you're seeing what you're seeing is because it's reaching out to models.dev for a list of models. This indicates that it's not set up in opencode to use a local model. 

Here's my set up.

in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` (you'd have to change it to your model name)
```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "lmstudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LM Studio (local)",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "mistral-7b-v0.1:2": {
          "name": "mistral-7b-v0.1:2"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

then open opencode 
- do `/models` and then press ctrl + a 
- type in local in which LM Studio (local) shows up. Press enter and then It prompts you for an API key and you just type in anything. Then when you should be able to see your models.

Then in your 99 configs, you pass in the model like this
```
require("99").setup({
    model = "mistral-7b-v0.1:2",
  })
```

Keep in mind, out of the box, opencode tries to send around 22k tokens off the bat so your model in LMStudio needs to be able to receive that. You can adjust this in the model settings and then you reload the model and start the server.

Hope this works for you.

